Git Intro –

Foundation of how modern teams collaborate, build, test and ship production

Version Control –

It’s a system that tracks code over time
Lets you undo, inspect and collaborate
Revert, review and branch safely
Essential for an DevOps/engineering workflow

Centralised (SVN) vs Distributed (Git) –

Centralised –

Single source of truth
Requires network to commit
Conflicts are common
Server outage = devs are stuck
Locks files during edits

Distributed –

Full history on every machine
Works offline
Fast branching and merging
Resilient to outages
Everyone can commit locally

Git History – 

Created by Linus Torvalds in 2005, the same person who created Linux
Built for Linux kernel Devs
Distributed: full history stored locally
Fast, powerful branching/merging
Default tool in almost every software team today
Popular in open source and companies

Git is NOT a File Tracker –

It tracks snapshots through commits and not differences
Uses a key-value store (SHA-1 hash)
Each commit = full snapshot of files
Files stored as objects (blobs, trees)
Content-addressed, not file-name based

Git Terminology –

Repository: a Git project (tracked folder with a git directory)
Commit: a snapshot of your files and metadata (author, timestamp, message etc)
Branch: a moveable pointer to a specific commit (main, dev, feature/login)
Remote: a reference to an external Git host (like Github or Gitlab) 
Staging Area: also called the index. It’s a buffer between your working directory and the repo what you plan to commit
Blobs: “binary large objects” – stores the actual content of files (just data not filename/paths)
Trees: stores filenames, file paths and pointers to blobs (files) and other trees (directories)
Ref (Reference): pointer to a commit: branches (refs/heads), tags (refs/tags) and special ones like HEAD
HEAD: pointer to the current branch or commit you’re working on
Index: the actual file .git/index, a binary file that holds the staging area info
Object Store: .git/objects/ - where Git stores all blobs, trees and commits by SHA hash
Tag: a ref to a specific commit, often used for marking releases

The .git Directory –

-	Contains all history and configuration

.git/refs/ - tags and branches
.git/objects/ - all your commits, blobs and trees
.git/config – repo specific settings
.git/HEAD – current branch pointer
.git/index – staging area

Git Common Commands –

Git init – initialises a new git repo with .git directory
Git add – stage changes
Git commit – snapshot changes
Git status – show staged/unstaged work
Git log – show commit history
Git diff – show what changed
Git config – set user/email
Git help <command> - built-in docs
Git clone – copy a remote repo
Git rm – remove files
Git mv – rename files
Git restore – undo file changes

The Areas of Git –

There’s 3 areas of Git: Working directory, Staging area, Repository
Working directory – files you/re editing
Staging area – Changes marked for commit
Repository – Commit snapshots

Viewing History –

You can use Git’s history using tools to trace back changes using essential commands such as git log or git log –oneline –graph which shows a visual summary.

Git vs GitHub –

Git is not GitHub. 
GitHub is a platform which is based online and Git runs locally.
Git is open source and nobody owns it but GitHub is owned by Microsoft.
Git tracks and manages code history on CLI whilst GitHub shares codes and manages codes.


Branching 101 –

Allows you to work on multiple things without messing up your main project. 
You can open a new branch if you want to try a new feature for example
Theres commands to use for branches:
Git branch – list/create branches
Git checkout -b <branch> - create and switch (older syntax)
Git switch -c <branch> - modern version
Git switch <branch> - switch branch safely
Git branch -d <branch> - delete branch

Merging –

Combines changes from one branch to another
Git merge <branch> - merges a target into current branch
Creates a merge commit
May cause conflict so manual resolution required

Visualise Branches and Logs –

Git log --online – shows a compact commit view
Git log --graph - visual tree structure
Git log --online --graph --all – compact, visual, full view
Great for debugging merges and tracking branches
Helps you see what’s really happening under the hood

Rebase vs Merge –

Merge: 
use when working collaboratively
preserves history
creates a new commit
good for team workflows

Rebase:
Use when cleaning up your local history
Rewrites history
No merge commits
Ideal for cleanup before PR

Git Stash & Pop –

Its great for scenarios where you’re not ready commit but need to move to another branch 

git stash – temporarily save uncommitted changes
git stash list – view all stashes 
git stash apply – reapply latest stash (keeps stash)
git stash pop – reapply and delete stash
Use when switching branches mid-task

Reset, Revert and Cherry-pick -

git revert –

creates a new commit that undoes another 
safe for shared history
used in production

git reset –

moves branch pointer backward
theres 3 types: 
soft = keeps changes
mixed = unstage
hard = nukes everything (be careful with this)

git cherry-pick –

apply a single commit from another branch
useful for hotfixes or targeted changes

Forks & Pull Requests –

Fork = your own copy of someone else’s repo (GitHub)
Clone the forked repo to your local machine
Make changes -> push to your fork
Open a Pull Request (PR) to propose your changes
Used in open source and cross-team workflows
Original repo owner can review, comment and merge

Collaborating Practices –

Uses branch to isolate work 
Push to remote and open PR
Assign reviewers, use GitHub’s UI for comments
Resolve conflicts before merge
Use issues, projects and discussions to track work
Keep commits focused and clean

Typical Git Workflow –

Developer pulls latest mains or clones repo
Creates feature branch
Work locally -> commits > pushes branch
Opens PR/MR -> review and merge
Team syncs regularly via git pull 



Trunk-Based Development –

Means all devs commit to main or short-lived branches
Heavy CI/testing gates
Used in fast-moving organisations (google, facebook etc)

Commit hygiene & Best Practices –

Write good commit messages
Use squashing before merging PRs
One logical change per commit
Avoid noisy merges 

Pre-Commit & Automation –

Run linters/tests before committing (pre-commit, Husky, tflint/sec etc)
Prevent broken code from entering the repo
Hook into CI pipelines for formatting, testing and scanning

Common Mistakes in the Real World –

Forgetting to pull before pushing 
Force pushing to shared branches (unless necessary)
Committing secrets
Merging without review 
Not using .gitignore properly. Not committing secrets or sensitive files

Git at Scale –

What happens in big organisations that use Git when your repo isn’t just a project and its thousands of devs involved:

Monorepo Strategies
Sparse checkout
Large file support (Git LFS)
Clean up legacy history (filter-branch, filter-repo)
Submodules vs subtrees in microservice repos
Selective CI builds Turborepo, Nx, Bazel etc)
Commit linting + bots to enforce rules
GitOps-style deployments (ArgoCD, Flux etc)
Server-side Git hooks (pre-commit.ci, Lefthook etc)






Git Security & Secrets Hygiene –

Preventing secret leaks in commits
Using git-secrets, trufflehog
Cleaning secrets from history
Auditing repo contributors + logs
